**Definition 1.4 (graphs and same-assignment whole potentials).** Let
$G=(V,E)$ be a connected simple 4-regular graph, $n=|V|\ge5$,
such that $G-v$ is connected for every $v\in V$. Thus $|E|=2n$.
Orient an Euler tour and fix the resulting two outgoing and two
incoming slots at every vertex, in that order, as $LL,LR,RL,RR$.
There is one shared bit $s_a\in\mathbb F_2$ for each edge $a$;
its two endpoint occurrences are the same variable. Its owner is its
terminal slot at the tail. Write $\operatorname{st}(v)$ for the four
incident edges. For fixed real parameters $A>B>0$, put

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi_v(z)&=\begin{cases}
A,&\sum_{a\in\operatorname{st}(v)}z_a=0\pmod2,\\
B,&\sum_{a\in\operatorname{st}(v)}z_a=1\pmod2,
\end{cases}\\
W(s)&=\prod_{v\in V}\Phi_v(s_{\operatorname{st}(v)}),
\qquad
Z_G=\sum_{s\in\mathbb F_2^E}W(s).
\end{aligned}
\tag{WBF.5}
$$

Each $\Phi_v$ is one entire original-block potential. It is not
replaced by independently weighted terminal or pairwise factors.

